 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479045 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479045 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e08e430c-ff42-53ba-a350-2d46855a44ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479049 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479049 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479049 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479049 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6ce6b76-7d56-5c1e-ab35-c635323e2536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/13822734ec2aa7810758b2aa3fd6ddd9972fdeb37d5aa2afaa796419f7327976.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(1.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19721c58-a18e-5cc3-8bbc-4270107e3b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479066 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[0,2].$ Notice that $x=1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/2d/2ddcaccc6d4b626001ea8179faabc9b298956ad5d7cce2461e6e8c3a4fb7754f.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0.5-2}{2-0}=\\frac{-1.5}{2}=-0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1.5)=-0.75.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/156dee2459824e3bfefa3b9e252b0f028ccea6d72c6a650808f51daa874bb35f.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cb27567-8cd1-5a11-9aa3-6be8d252ac76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479073 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/69/690bfc52663b966da350e6fa941057a7b20a2d9547d39cd95782059ee3c44aba.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-0}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ff/ff4df5dce9f78798b864ea1880990cb91af83c7ec5fd1ec37775d885f72bc250.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3156e798-219c-5353-a742-46b2b157915b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479076 #edb/ref 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=0$ and $g(1)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,5)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,2)$ equals $-3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-3$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/5981116f8c9ad6e9dea29da381a0becbd997aa259a232dd10bd7395a78e976ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(-1)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3187c568-64de-59e3-811a-985d87754a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479077 #edb/ref 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(-1)={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=1$ and $h(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${h}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a6/a6b4c982c1bcc4952ab74979e7d226b6c61faa2d79fbdbef9673e263acd0ff80.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5467eedc-6dd1-5de2-a836-5a3460561247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479091 #edb/ref 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/0115d90518526da279492096da66497c3ea1e98cbff14e66657bb08c11ea479b.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "791eebb0-2b19-5413-9776-2b792618848a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479099 #edb/ref 1074 "The product and addition rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=2$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/625de64447d959dab5a8a0da610e8687603454c331bb1c257300f222ab2e7f10.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=3f(x)-4g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8879cd1c-dff5-51df-b6a6-3a39f9ea79bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479103 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=3{f}^{′}(x)-4{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d90d88b1e2cff1e08017bde2dd8d615444187f0290704e077f974ec58ee46112.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-2}{0-(-4)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23d22833ae89d7ed3f1c34a07f039031d77223350f0b23d17c6843f67f05668c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-5g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abfb0f27-f94d-54ac-a8fa-eb380c163f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479114 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-5{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de80fcae8f07412e788df9ef202b15f5cdcf9fe8ca9a994abd8b1acd789359f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{3-1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b6/b63554ba2724862e30569687e53e2068eda0024052d49a163b6aa2097c2ba659.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b26b207d-effc-596c-ae01-16ed4d0fdbd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479118 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[2,4].$ Notice that $x=3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/35/357d426d58f6dd77a618fd30ad3735ef6a77763f77387be3181bd5149f973488.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-5-(-1)}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d23f912d6cc6614de82fe27d1911b32fd0faa7e29bd61a9549797cfabebb3458.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=r(x)$ and $y=s(x)$ are given above. Find ${q}^{′}(3)$ where $q(x)=r(x)s(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba0c1033-2092-580d-8cc6-92bb02b8740a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479120 #edb/ref 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)={r}^{′}(x)\\cdot s(x)+r(x)\\cdot {s}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(3)={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs, we obtain that $r(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s(3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $r(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${r}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $s(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $1.$ So, ${s}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(3) & ={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e78aafab1786a1fa7ee8e58c80c41b246179cfcce7399324731131f40e8d1490.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e322fcd2-b2f5-5ca5-b94b-ad3cb0222a99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479136 #edb/ref 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=-1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fa/fa4b5ffe6c0abfa7ca5583fe2304d1acc2d35fa5d8949bf9526b2efd296316cd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc313451-9f12-5322-b234-e99e208aefa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479146 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(-2),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-3,0].$ Notice that $x=-2$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/63/63ea83d99ec5e3ba226fea092134d8289e0e88f0b0d426a99a9b69f7fe5abe75.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-2)}{0-(-3)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
